---
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const title = "BC PNP Statistical Report 2025: What the Numbers Reveal and What They Mean for 2027";
const description = "Analysis of the BC PNP Statistical Report and Year in Review 2025: 6,214 nominations, only 978 ITAs, healthcare growth, wages, regions and source countries.";
const canonical = "https://bcpnpcalculator.ca/articles/bc-pnp-statistical-report-2025-analysis";

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How many people did the BC PNP nominate in 2025?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The BC PNP used its full 2025 allocation of 6,214 nominations: 6,195 through Skills Immigration and 19 through Entrepreneur Immigration. The allocation started at 4,000 and was later increased by the federal government."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How many invitations to apply did the BC PNP issue in 2025?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The report says the BC PNP issued 978 Skills Immigration invitations to apply in 2025, and 748 of them led to applications, a conversion rate of 76.5%. Most 2025 nominations came from applications already in inventory, especially International Post-Graduate applications submitted before that stream closed."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What was the median wage of BC PNP nominees in 2025?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The median wage of Skills Immigration nominees was $41.42 per hour, compared with a median of $32.49 per hour for all B.C. workers. Skilled Worker stream nominees had a median salary of $105,000 and Health Authority nominees a median of $80,769."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What share of BC PNP nominees settled outside Metro Vancouver in 2025?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "38% of Skills Immigration nominees in 2025 were based outside the Metro Vancouver Regional District, above the program's target of at least 35%. All 19 Entrepreneur Immigration nominees were outside Metro Vancouver."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which countries did most BC PNP nominees come from in 2025?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The top five source countries for Skills Immigration nominees in 2025 were India, China, Iran, the Philippines and the United States. Together, East and Southeast Asia and South and Central Asia accounted for 63.2% of nominees."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "When was the BC PNP Statistical Report 2025 released?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "WelcomeBC announced the BC PNP Statistical Report and Year in Review 2025 on August 26, 2026. It is a 33-page PDF covering allocations, invitations, nominations by stream, occupations, wages, source countries, regional distribution and Entrepreneur Immigration."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<ArticleLayout slug="bc-pnp-statistical-report-2025-analysis" datePublished="2026-10-14" dateModified="2026-10-14" articleSection="Analysis" keywords="BC PNP statistical report 2025, BC PNP year in review 2025, BC PNP nominations 2025, BC PNP data, BC PNP 2027" title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="inline-block bg-indigo-100 text-indigo-800 px-4 py-1 rounded-full text-sm font-bold mb-4 border border-indigo-200 shadow-sm">
        Data Analysis
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        BC PNP Statistical Report 2025: What the Numbers Reveal and What They Mean for 2027
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-4">
        The province's official year-end report shows a program that nominated 6,214 people while issuing fewer than 1,000 invitations. We pulled out the figures that matter for anyone planning a 2027 application.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <time datetime="2026-10-14">October 14, 2026</time>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>11 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-indigo-600 mb-2">6,214</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Nominations in 2025 (full allocation used)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-green-600 mb-2">978</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Skills Immigration ITAs issued in 2025</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-red-600 mb-2">38%</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">SI nominees outside Metro Vancouver</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        On August 26, 2026, WelcomeBC published the <strong>BC PNP Statistical Report and Year in Review 2025</strong>, a 33-page document covering allocations, invitations, nominations by stream, occupations, wages, source countries, regional distribution and Entrepreneur Immigration. Every figure in this article comes from that report unless we say otherwise. Where we add 2026 data, it comes from WelcomeBC's news and invitations pages, and where we interpret, we label it as analysis.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">2025 in One Paragraph</h2>

      <p>
        The report calls 2025 "a challenging year." BC's initial allocation was cut to 4,000, half of the 8,000 it received in 2024. At the same time, a late-2024 surge of International Post-Graduate (IPG) applications left the program starting the year with about 5,200 applications in inventory, more than the whole allocation. The BC PNP put IPG files on hold for an extended period and restricted new intake to healthcare professionals and a limited number of "high economic impact" workers. A later federal top-up raised the allocation to 6,214, which let the program clear much of its inventory. The report says almost 90% of nominees were already living and working in B.C.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Nominations by Program and Stream</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Component</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2022</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2023</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2024</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2025</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Skills Immigration</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,966</td><td class="px-4 py-3">8,168</td><td class="px-4 py-3">7,972</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,195</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Entrepreneur Immigration</td><td class="px-4 py-3">34</td><td class="px-4 py-3">32</td><td class="px-4 py-3">28</td><td class="px-4 py-3">19</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-slate-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">Total</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">7,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">8,200</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">8,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">6,214</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The stream breakdown is where 2025 looks unusual. Half of all Skills Immigration nominations went to graduate streams that no longer accept applications.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Stream</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2023</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2024</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2025</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Skilled Worker</td><td class="px-4 py-3">996</td><td class="px-4 py-3">656</td><td class="px-4 py-3">284</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">EEBC – Skilled Worker</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2,800</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2,162</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,106</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Health Authority</td><td class="px-4 py-3">473</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,430</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,176</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">EEBC – Health Authority</td><td class="px-4 py-3">246</td><td class="px-4 py-3">395</td><td class="px-4 py-3">502</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">International Post-Graduate</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,055</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,001</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,991</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">EEBC – International Post-Graduate</td><td class="px-4 py-3">664</td><td class="px-4 py-3">487</td><td class="px-4 py-3">557</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">International Graduate</td><td class="px-4 py-3">369</td><td class="px-4 py-3">521</td><td class="px-4 py-3">193</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">EEBC – International Graduate</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,140</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,097</td><td class="px-4 py-3">349</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Entry Level and Semi-Skilled</td><td class="px-4 py-3">425</td><td class="px-4 py-3">223</td><td class="px-4 py-3">37</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-slate-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">SI total</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">8,168</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">7,972</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">6,195</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        By the report's own breakdown, 22% of 2025 Skills Immigration nominations were Skilled Worker, 27% Health Authority and 50% International Graduate or International Post-Graduate. Adding up the four EEBC rows, we calculate that 2,514 nominees (about 41%) used the Express Entry BC option. The report describes the 2025 mix as "an unusual departure from previous years."
      </p>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-2">Key Takeaway</p>
        <p class="text-blue-800">IPG nominees made up 41.1% of 2025 Skills Immigration nominees, and the IPG stream closed in early January 2025. That inventory is now largely cleared, so the 2025 stream shares are not a guide to 2026 or 2027. Expect Skilled Worker and Health Authority to carry nearly all future nominations.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The Invitation Bottleneck: 978 ITAs</h2>

      <p>
        This is the most striking number in the report. In all of 2025, the BC PNP issued just <strong>978 Skills Immigration invitations to apply</strong>. Of those, 748 became applications, a 76.5% conversion rate. Nominations far exceeded invitations because the Health Authority and IPG streams do not require a registration or invitation, and because the program was processing files submitted in late 2024.
      </p>

      <p>
        Decisions tell the same story. The BC PNP made 6,553 Skills Immigration decisions in 2025, down 25.4% from 8,784 in 2024, and 6,195 of them were nominations, an approval rate of 94.5%. Meanwhile, the report's chart of active registrations shows the pool climbing back to roughly 11,000 by late 2025, after dropping when the International Graduate part of the pool closed in November 2024.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Healthcare: The Big Winner</h2>

      <p>
        Healthcare grew even as the allocation shrank. The report says healthcare professionals nominated while working for a public health authority rose to <strong>1,241 in 2025</strong>, an 84% increase over 675 in 2024. It also reports 218 physicians and 652 registered nurses and nurse practitioners nominated in 2025.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Clinical health nominations</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2023</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2024</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2025</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Working for a health authority</td><td class="px-4 py-3">376</td><td class="px-4 py-3">641</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,180</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Working for other employers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">403</td><td class="px-4 py-3">698</td><td class="px-4 py-3">194</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-slate-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">Total</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">779</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">1,339</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">1,374</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">% of SI nominees</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9.5%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">16.8%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">22.2%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        You will see slightly different healthcare totals in different parts of the report (1,241 versus 1,180 for health authority workers, and 1,435 "prioritized healthcare occupations" in the performance measures). The report explains that its healthcare definitions differ from NOC-based categories; treat each figure as a separate measure rather than an error. The important trend is consistent: <strong>private-sector health employers lost ground sharply</strong> (698 to 194) while health authority employees nearly doubled.
      </p>

      <p>
        International recruitment also shows up in the data. From the United Kingdom, physician nominees rose 139% and registered nurse nominees 17%. From the United States, physician nominees rose 290% and registered nurse nominees 208%. Including nurse practitioners, 335 physicians, nurses and nurse practitioners were nominated from those two countries alone. For the full Care occupation picture, see our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-health-authority-stream" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Health Authority stream guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Other Priorities: Childcare, Vets, Construction and Tech</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Priority group</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2023</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2024</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2025</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Childcare</td><td class="px-4 py-3">842</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,279</td><td class="px-4 py-3">409</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Veterinary care</td><td class="px-4 py-3">41</td><td class="px-4 py-3">48</td><td class="px-4 py-3">25</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Construction (prioritized from Nov 2023)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">64</td><td class="px-4 py-3">496</td><td class="px-4 py-3">266</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Priority technology</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2,490</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,302</td><td class="px-4 py-3">607</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The report credits the Tech Pilot, launched in 2017, and the priority-technology measures that followed it with 13,868 technology nominations between 2017 and 2025, but tech's share of Skills Immigration nominees fell from 30.4% in 2023 to 9.8% in 2025. It notes that many technology workers were instead included in High Economic Impact draws introduced in 2025. Childcare's drop from 1,279 to 409 reflects, in the report's words, a "temporary intensification" of ECE prioritization in 2024 to meet an acute labour gap.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Occupations: Health Rises, Sales and Service Collapse</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Occupational category (share of SI nominees)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2023</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2024</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2025</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">No listed occupation (IPG)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">21.0%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">18.7%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">41.1%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Health</td><td class="px-4 py-3">7.2%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">12.1%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">20.2%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Education, law and social, community and government services</td><td class="px-4 py-3">15.0%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">23.5%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">12.1%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Natural and applied sciences</td><td class="px-4 py-3">29.1%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">16.3%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">10.5%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Business, finance and administration</td><td class="px-4 py-3">7.7%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">8.0%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5.3%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Trades, transport and equipment operators</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2.9%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">7.4%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5.3%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Sales and service</td><td class="px-4 py-3">11.0%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">12.0%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">4.0%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The smaller categories (senior management, arts and culture, manufacturing and utilities, and natural resources) were each under 1% in 2025. The fall in business, administration and sales and service foreshadowed the <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-ineligible-occupations-employers-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">ineligible occupations list</a> introduced in the 2026 program guide.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Wages: Nominees Now Earn Well Above the B.C. Median</h2>

      <p>
        The median wage of Skills Immigration nominees was <strong>$41.42 per hour</strong> in 2025, $12.42 higher than in 2024 and $8.93 above the B.C. median of $32.49. The report attributes the jump to fewer ECE nominations (a lower-paid occupation), more physicians and nurses, and High Economic Impact draws.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Stream</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Average 2025 salary</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Median 2025 salary</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Skilled Worker</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$125,251</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$105,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Health Authority</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$115,243</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$80,769</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">International Graduate</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$60,699</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$57,200</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Entry Level and Semi-Skilled</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$44,722</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$42,685</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-slate-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">All streams except IPG</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">$110,236</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">$80,769</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        IPG nominees are excluded because that stream did not require a job offer. The report also says over 99% of nominees with a job offer earned more than the provincial minimum wage, and that B.C.'s nominees have strong outcomes: among permanent residents admitted in 2022, BC nominees' median 2023 income was $13,600 higher than provincial nominees nationally, and 97% still lived in B.C. after one year (92% after five).
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Where Nominees Came From</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">World region (2025 SI nominees)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Nominees</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">East and Southeast Asia</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2,111</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">South and Central Asia</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,807</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">North Africa and Middle East</td><td class="px-4 py-3">520</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Europe</td><td class="px-4 py-3">499</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Sub-Saharan Africa</td><td class="px-4 py-3">477</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Latin America and Caribbean</td><td class="px-4 py-3">407</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">United States</td><td class="px-4 py-3">313</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Oceania</td><td class="px-4 py-3">61</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The top five individual source countries were India, China, Iran, the Philippines and, for the first time in the three years shown, the <strong>United States</strong>, which replaced Nigeria (2024) and Brazil (2023) in fifth place. That reflects the healthcare recruitment drive described above.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Regional Distribution</h2>

      <p>
        38% of Skills Immigration nominees were based outside the Metro Vancouver Regional District, beating the program's 35% target. The report says this was a modest decrease from 2024, caused by the large number of IPG nominees, who were concentrated in Metro Vancouver.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Development region</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Health</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">SW</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">IPG</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Total</th>
