            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Mainland/Southwest</td><td class="px-4 py-3">870</td><td class="px-4 py-3">823</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2,207</td><td class="px-4 py-3">4,018</td><td class="px-4 py-3">64.9%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Vancouver Island/Coast</td><td class="px-4 py-3">281</td><td class="px-4 py-3">291</td><td class="px-4 py-3">203</td><td class="px-4 py-3">964</td><td class="px-4 py-3">15.6%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Thompson/Okanagan and Kootenay</td><td class="px-4 py-3">254</td><td class="px-4 py-3">201</td><td class="px-4 py-3">99</td><td class="px-4 py-3">756</td><td class="px-4 py-3">12.2%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">North (Cariboo, Nechako, North Coast, Northeast)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">273</td><td class="px-4 py-3">75</td><td class="px-4 py-3">39</td><td class="px-4 py-3">457</td><td class="px-4 py-3">7.4%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">Totals also include International Graduate and ELSS nominees, which are not shown as separate columns.</p>

      <p>
        Metro Vancouver received 3,836 nominees, led by the City of Vancouver (1,700, about 27% of all SI nominees), Burnaby (852), Surrey (415) and Richmond (284). Outside the Mainland/Southwest, Thompson/Okanagan had 647, the Cariboo 198, the Northeast 118, the North Coast 115, the Kootenay 109 and Nechako 26.
      </p>

      <p>
        One detail stands out for 2027 planning. Health stream nominees were split almost evenly: 870 in the Mainland/Southwest and 808 elsewhere. And if we remove IPG nominees from the totals, our calculation is that roughly half of the remaining nominees lived outside the Mainland/Southwest region. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-look-west-regional-nominations-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Look West regional nominations guide</a> covers how regional points work.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Entrepreneur Immigration and Program Integrity</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Base stream:</strong> 191 registrations in 2025 (up 71% from 112), of which 106 qualified for the pool and 95 received ITAs.</li>
        <li><strong>Regional stream:</strong> 11 registrations, 9 qualified and 9 invited.</li>
        <li><strong>Outcomes:</strong> 65 applications received, 39 work permit stage approvals and 19 nominations, all outside Metro Vancouver. The 2025 nominees created 42 jobs and invested almost $5 million. Over 2016 to 2025, EI nominees established 394 businesses and created 1,306 jobs.</li>
        <li><strong>Anti-fraud:</strong> by the end of 2025, the BC PNP's Anti-Fraud and Investigations Unit had conducted more than 2,000 case reviews or investigations. See our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-job-offer-scams-fraud-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">job offer fraud guide</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The 2026 Targets the Report Sets</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Measure</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2025 result</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">2026 target</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">SI nominees outside Metro Vancouver</td><td class="px-4 py-3">38%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">At least 35%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">EI nominees outside Metro Vancouver</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100%</td><td class="px-4 py-3">At least 60%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Nominees in prioritized healthcare occupations</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,435</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,620 to 2,015</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Early childhood educator nominees</td><td class="px-4 py-3">409</td><td class="px-4 py-3">360 to 440</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Median wage of SI nominees</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$41.42/hour</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Within a reasonable range of the B.C. median</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What Has Already Changed in 2026</h2>

      <p>
        The report looks backward, but 2026 has moved fast. On WelcomeBC's pages: the 2026 allocation started at 5,254 and rose to <strong>6,254</strong> on August 18; on April 23 the program reorganized around Care, Build and Innovate; and the invitations page lists at least <strong>6,297 Skills Immigration invitations</strong> between February 4 and October 8, 2026 (our sum, excluding rounds shown as "fewer than 5"). By our count that breaks down to about 3,974 High Economic Impact, 1,439 Care, 791 Build and 93 rural/remote health invitations. The pool stood at 7,695 registrations on October 6, 2026.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-green-50 border-l-4 border-green-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-green-900 mb-2">Context</p>
        <p class="text-green-800">978 invitations in all of 2025 versus more than 6,000 in roughly eight months of 2026 is the single clearest sign that the bottleneck has shifted. In 2025 the constraint was inventory; in 2026 it is competition among registrants for targeted draws.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What It Means for 2027 (Our Analysis)</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Healthcare stays central.</strong> The 2026 healthcare target range of 1,620 to 2,015 is above 2025's 1,435. If past patterns hold, health authority employees will remain the most reliable route, and private-sector healthcare roles will depend on targeted Care draws.</li>
        <li><strong>The regional share should rise, not fall.</strong> The report itself blames the dip to 38% on Metro-concentrated IPG nominees. With IPG gone, we expect the outside-Metro share to edge up, which is consistent with the April 2026 commitment that at least 35% of nominations go outside Metro Vancouver.</li>
        <li><strong>Wages will stay high.</strong> With ELSS and graduate streams closed and High Economic Impact draws using wage thresholds between $52 and $70 per hour in 2026, we expect median nominee wages to stay well above the B.C. median.</li>
        <li><strong>The allocation is the swing factor.</strong> No 2027 allocation has been announced. The federal 2026–2028 levels plan set a national PNP target of 92,500 for 2027, but the 2027–2029 plan expected in November 2026 could change that. See our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-2027-outlook-predictions" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">2027 BC PNP outlook</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How to Use These Numbers</h2>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Find your lane.</strong> If you are in a Care or Build occupation, your competition is the targeted draw for that group. If not, you are competing on wage or points in High Economic Impact draws.</li>
        <li><strong>Benchmark your wage.</strong> A Skilled Worker median of $105,000 in 2025 tells you where High Economic Impact candidates sit. If your offer is far below that and you are not in a priority occupation, focus on raising your score.</li>
        <li><strong>Consider location.</strong> Regional points and a stated 35% regional target make a genuine job outside Metro Vancouver a real advantage.</li>
        <li><strong>Check your score.</strong> The <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a> shows where you stand against 2026 cutoffs.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">How many people did the BC PNP nominate in 2025?</h3>
          <p>The BC PNP used its full 2025 allocation of 6,214 nominations: 6,195 through Skills Immigration and 19 through Entrepreneur Immigration. The allocation started at 4,000 and was later increased by the federal government.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">How many invitations to apply did the BC PNP issue in 2025?</h3>
          <p>The report says the BC PNP issued 978 Skills Immigration invitations to apply in 2025, and 748 of them led to applications, a conversion rate of 76.5%. Most 2025 nominations came from applications already in inventory, especially International Post-Graduate applications submitted before that stream closed.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">What was the median wage of BC PNP nominees in 2025?</h3>
          <p>The median wage of Skills Immigration nominees was $41.42 per hour, compared with a median of $32.49 per hour for all B.C. workers. Skilled Worker stream nominees had a median salary of $105,000 and Health Authority nominees a median of $80,769.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">What share of BC PNP nominees settled outside Metro Vancouver in 2025?</h3>
          <p>38% of Skills Immigration nominees in 2025 were based outside the Metro Vancouver Regional District, above the program's target of at least 35%. All 19 Entrepreneur Immigration nominees were outside Metro Vancouver.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Which countries did most BC PNP nominees come from in 2025?</h3>
          <p>The top five source countries for Skills Immigration nominees in 2025 were India, China, Iran, the Philippines and the United States. Together, East and Southeast Asia and South and Central Asia accounted for 63.2% of nominees.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">When was the BC PNP Statistical Report 2025 released?</h3>
          <p>WelcomeBC announced the BC PNP Statistical Report and Year in Review 2025 on August 26, 2026. It is a 33-page PDF covering allocations, invitations, nominations by stream, occupations, wages, source countries, regional distribution and Entrepreneur Immigration.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

